    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We write the absolute value as two cases. We then re-express both cases in an appropriate form. We then set up two linear equations and solve. \\begin{align*}\n\\text{Case 1:} & \\quad\\quad\\quad n + 6 = 2 - n \\\\\n& n + n  = 2 - 6\\\\\n2n &= -4\\\\\nn &= - 2\n\\end{align*}\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Case 2:} & \\quad\\quad\\quad n + 6 = -(2 - n) \\\\\n& n + 6 = -2 + n \\\\\nn + n &= -2 - 6 \\\\\n2n &= -8 \\\\\nn & = -4\n\\end{align*}\nI find something wrong with the reasoning. First I conclude that $n < 2$.\nThen I find two solutions from the two cases.\n\nBut why? You should be getting $n=0$. Please fix your solution.\n$n + 6 = -2 + n \\implies n = -8 \\ne -4$\n\nYou can't substitute it like that\nBy considering all cases on the other side, we get two cases: $2-n \\geq 0$ and $2-n \\leq 0$.\nSo,\n$|n+6| = 2-n$ <==> $n+6=2-n$ and $n+6=-2+n$\nSolving $n+6=2-n$, $n=-2$, and $n=-4$\nSolving $n+6=-2+n, n=-8$\nHence, the correct answer should be $n=-2$, or $n=-4$ or $n=-8$\n\nThats a nice example\n\nYou shouldn't have written the inequality $n+6=2-n$. If you did then it would have helped demonstrate that absolute values are not simply $+$, but also $-$. And it also shows how the absolute value of a number is its distance from zero.",
